+*   Gradient definition for a multi-valued 3D field $\Phi(u_1, u_2, u_3) : \mathfrak{R}^3 \rightarrow \mathfrak{R}^m$, where $m$ is the number of data channels per sample point [S3]. The difference between two spatial points $P = (u_1^0, u_2^0, u_3^0)$ and $Q = (u_1^1, u_2^1, u_3^1)$ is $\Delta\Phi = \Phi(P) - \Phi(Q)$ [S3]. As the distance between $P$ and $Q$ tends to zero, the differential becomes $d\Phi = \sum_{i=1}^3 \frac{\delta\Phi}{\delta u_i} du_i$ [S3]. The squared norm is $d\Phi^2 = \sum_{i=1}^3 \sum_{j=1}^3 \frac{\delta\Phi}{\delta u_i} \cdot \frac{\delta\Phi}{\delta u_j} du_i du_j$ [S3].
+*   Metric tensor formulation: Define $g_{ij} = \frac{\delta\Phi}{\delta u_i} \cdot \frac{\delta\Phi}{\delta u_j}$ as the metric tensor describing the rate of change in all directions [S3]. Then $d\Phi^2 = \sum_{i=1}^3 \sum_{j=1}^3 g_{ij} du_i du_j = \begin{bmatrix} du_1 \\ du_2 \\ du_3 \end{bmatrix}^T \begin{bmatrix} g_{11} & g_{12} & g_{13} \\ g_{21} & g_{22} & g_{23} \\ g_{31} & g_{32} & g_{33} \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} du_1 \\ du_2 \\ du_3 \end{bmatrix}$ [S3].
+*   Weighting heuristic: To handle different dynamic ranges across channels, each data channel's derivative is multiplied by a weight constant $w_k = 1 / (max_k - min_k)$, where $max_k$ and $min_k$ are the maximum and minimum data values for channel $k$ [S3]. This is expressed as a scale matrix $W$ (an $m \times m$ diagonal matrix) [S3]:
     $d\Phi = W \left( \sum_{i=1}^3 \frac{\delta\Phi}{\delta u_i} du_i \right), \quad W := \begin{bmatrix} w_1 & 0 & 0 & \cdots \\ 0 & w_2 & 0 & \cdots \\ & & \ddots & \\ 0 & 0 & \cdots & w_m \end{bmatrix}$ [S3].
 
